    "description": "Environmental technology connects measurements, models, and operational decisions about water, agriculture, pollution, and ecosystems. Documented Iranian applications include satellite-assisted water accounting in the Lake Urmia basin, agricultural drought monitoring, and experimental evaluation of inexpensive pollution sensors.",
    "tags": ["Environmental technology", "Remote sensing", "Water accounting", "Drought monitoring", "Pollution sensors", "Environmental data"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T06:47:27Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T06:47:27Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Environmental Technology in Iran: Sensors, Satellites and Water Data",
      "description": "Explore Iranian environmental technology through Lake Urmia water accounting, agricultural drought monitoring and pollution-sensor research."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "measurement-and-action",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From environmental signals to decisions",
        "content": "Environmental technology becomes useful when a measurement can answer a management question. In Iran, documented examples connect satellite observations with water accounts and crop-stress assessments, while pollution research examines how inexpensive sensors can complement conventional instruments. These applications involve different kinds of evidence: a satellite-derived estimate, a calibrated sensor reading, and a management decision should not be treated as interchangeable. [s1][s3][s6]\n\nThe field spans several connected branches. Pollution sensors observe conditions at particular locations; remote sensing gathers information without direct contact with the surface; satellite monitoring follows conditions through repeated observations. Water-management systems and precision agriculture use interpreted information to support choices, while environmental data analytics organizes and evaluates the evidence behind those choices. The Lake Urmia examples show how several branches can operate together rather than as isolated technologies. [s1][s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3", "s4", "s6"]
      },
      {
        "id": "water-accounting",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Water accounting around Lake Urmia",
        "content": "Water Accounting Plus, commonly written WA+, brings water-related information into a structured basin account. FAO documents its use in the Lake Urmia basin, in northwestern Iran, with remotely sensed and other open-access data used to compute water flows. The documented assessment covers reporting periods running from October 2013 to September 2014 and October 2014 to September 2015. These are specific hydrological reporting periods, not observations for the calendar years shown in a publication date. [s1]\n\nIHE Delft identifies the assessment as part of an FAO-led sustainable-water-management programme supporting the Urmia Lake Restoration Program. Its listed outputs include geospatial data and a report. That institutional connection establishes the study's intended role; it does not independently prove that the assessment restored lake levels or changed water allocations. [s2]\n\nA separate scholarly study applied WA+ to the western part of the basin for 2010–2016, combining remotely sensed information with a distributed hydrological model. This illustrates environmental analytics as a method of assembling incomplete information, rather than simply displaying satellite pictures. Its study area and historical period also set limits on generalization to other Iranian basins. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "agricultural-monitoring",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Agricultural monitoring needs local calibration",
        "content": "FAO's Agricultural Stress Index System, or ASIS, uses satellite information to identify cropped areas potentially affected by drought. In a 2020 account of work with Iran, FAO emphasized calibration using land-use maps, sowing dates, crop-cycle duration, and crop coefficients. A vegetation signal therefore acquires agricultural meaning through knowledge of what is growing and when. [s4]\n\nThat account described an initial focus on the Urmia basin and an intended expansion to national coverage. The UN's subsequent report on 2021 activities stated that water-accounting and agricultural-stress-monitoring work had been transferred to Iranian experts. These records document training and institutional handover; they do not provide an independently evaluated national service-performance record. [s4][s5]\n\nFAO's country-level ASIS documentation explains that the tool summarizes calibrated observations into maps for decision-makers. Its public catalogue lists Iran with internal access only. This is a reminder that a documented monitoring capability and a publicly downloadable data service are different things; the catalogue alone cannot establish the present quality or completeness of Iranian outputs. [s7]",
        "source_ids": ["s4", "s5", "s7"]
      },
      {
        "id": "sensors-and-quality",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Pollution sensors and the quality of evidence",
        "content": "A field study reported in 2024 evaluated inexpensive particulate-matter sensors alongside a standard urban monitoring station during October–December 2023. The inspected abstract describes measurements of fine and coarse particles together with temperature and humidity, and concludes that appropriately calibrated sensors can supplement regulatory instruments. This supports their potential for denser local observation; it does not establish equivalence under every season or pollution mixture. [s6]\n\nThe common lesson across water accounting, crop monitoring, and pollution sensing is that interpretation and validation matter as much as acquiring hardware. Local calibration, explicit reporting periods, and clear geographic scope make results more useful. Evaluation should then ask what decisions followed and whether environmental outcomes changed. The sources here document tools and studies more strongly than they document sustained improvements attributable to those tools. [s1][s3][s4][s6]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3", "s4", "s6"]
      },
      {
        "id": "agricultural-information-process",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "How agricultural observations become decision support",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"Satellite observations\"] -->|\"Provide environmental signals\"| C[\"Locally calibrated ASIS analysis\"]\n  B[\"Crop calendars and land-use information\"] -->|\"Provide local calibration inputs\"| C\n  C -->|\"Summarizes potential crop water stress\"| D[\"Agricultural stress maps\"]\n  D -->|\"Inform assessment of drought risk\"| E[\"Management decisions\"]\n  E -->|\"Require separate evaluation\"| F[\"Environmental and agricultural outcomes\"]\n```",
        "summary": "FAO describes satellite data and local crop information as inputs to agricultural-stress analysis and decision-support maps. The final arrow identifies an evaluation requirement, not a claim that the documented Iranian work achieved measured improvements.",
        "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
        "source_ids": ["s4", "s7"]
      }
    ],
